{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":28755,"isInternetEnabled":false,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/rag-retrieval-augmented-generation?scriptVersionId=366341039\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 1. What is RAG?\n\nRAG stands for **Retrieval-Augmented Generation**.\n\nIt is a technique that combines information retrieval with language generation.\n\nIn RAG, the model first retrieves relevant information from an external knowledge source and then uses that information as context to generate an answer.\n\nInstead of depending only on the knowledge learned during training, an LLM can use external documents to answer questions.\n\n### Why do we use RAG?\n\n1. **Up-to-date information:** We can provide the model with updated information without retraining it.\n2. **Access to private data:** We can connect the model to our own documents, PDFs, company data, etc.\n3. **More grounded answers:** The model can generate answers using the retrieved information as context.\n4. **Large document collections:** We can work with large collections of documents by retrieving only the relevant parts instead of sending everything to the LLM.\n\n**Important:** RAG does not remove the LLM's context-window limit. It helps us select relevant information so that we do not need to send the entire document collection to the model.\n\n## 2. Four Main Components of RAG\n\nThe four important components we will study are:\n\n1. **Document Loaders:** Load data from different sources and convert it into a common format.\n2. **Text Splitters:** Divide large documents into smaller chunks.\n3. **Vector Stores:** Store document chunks and their vector embeddings so that they can be searched efficiently.\n4. **Retrievers:** Find and return relevant documents or chunks based on a user's query.\n\n### Basic RAG Workflow\n\n1. Document Loaders load data from sources such as PDFs, text files, websites, and CSV files.\n2. Text Splitters divide large documents into smaller chunks.\n3. An embedding model converts these chunks into numerical vectors.\n4. Vector Stores store the chunks and their embeddings.\n5. A Retriever searches for the most relevant chunks when a user asks a question.\n6. The retrieved information is passed to the LLM as context.\n7. The LLM generates the final answer.\n\n---\n\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Document Loaders in LangChain\n\n#  Document Loaders\n\n## What are Document Loaders?\n\nDocument Loaders are components in LangChain used to load data from different sources and convert it into a standard format called a **Document object**.\n\n### Why do we need Document Loaders?\n\nIn real-world applications, data can come from different sources and formats, such as:\n\n- PDF files\n- Text files\n- CSV files\n- Websites\n- Word documents\n- Multiple files stored in a directory\n\nEach source has a different format and structure.\n\nTo make these different data sources work consistently with the other components of LangChain, we convert them into a common format called a Document object.\n\n## 4. Document Object\n\nA **Document object** is a standard data structure used by LangChain to store text content along with additional information about that content.\n\nEvery Document object mainly contains two things:\n\n### 1. page_content\n\n- Contains the actual textual content extracted from the source.\n- For example, the text extracted from a PDF page or a row in a CSV file.\n\n### 2. metadata\n\n- Contains additional information about the document.\n- It helps us identify where the content came from and provides useful details about it.\n- Examples: source file name, page number, row number, etc.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA Document object created from a PDF page may contain:\n\n- `page_content`: The actual text extracted from that page.\n- `metadata`: Information such as the PDF file name and page number.\n\n**Important:** A Document object is not the original PDF or text file. It is LangChain's standard representation of the extracted content and its metadata.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Types of Document Loaders\n\n## A. TextLoader\n\n**Definition:** TextLoader is used to load plain-text files and convert their content into Document objects.\n\n- It is commonly used with `.txt` files.\n- It generally creates one Document object for the entire text file.\n- The extracted text is stored in `page_content`.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.document_loaders import TextLoader\n\nloader = TextLoader(\"example.txt\")\n\ndocs = loader.load()\n```\n\nHere:\n- `TextLoader()` creates the loader.\n- `load()` loads the file and returns a list of Document objects.\n\n**Note:** TextLoader is designed for plain-text content. It is not a general-purpose loader for every file format.\n\n## B. PyPDFLoader\n\n**Definition:** PyPDFLoader is used to load and extract text from PDF files.\n\n- It uses the `pypdf` library internally.\n- It generally creates one Document object for each PDF page.\n- Each Document object contains the text extracted from that page along with its metadata.\n- It works best with PDFs that already contain selectable text.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.document_loaders import PyPDFLoader\n\nloader = PyPDFLoader(\"example.pdf\")\n\ndocs = loader.load()\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nIf a PDF contains 5 pages, PyPDFLoader generally returns a list containing 5 Document objects.\n\n### Limitations of PyPDFLoader\n\n- It is not suitable for extracting text from scanned PDFs that contain only page images.\n- It may not preserve complex layouts, tables, or columns accurately.\n- It mainly extracts the textual content of a PDF.\n\n### Other PDF Loaders\n\nDifferent PDF loaders are useful for different types of PDFs.\n\n| Use Case | Recommended Loader |\n|---|---|\n| Simple, clean PDFs | PyPDFLoader |\n| PDFs with tables and columns | PDFPlumberLoader or PDFPlumber-based processing |\n| Scanned or image-based PDFs | UnstructuredPDFLoader or AmazonTextractPDFLoader |\n| PDFs where layout and image data matter | PyMuPDFLoader |\n| More advanced document structure extraction | UnstructuredPDFLoader |\n\n**Important:** These loaders use different extraction methods. The best choice depends on the PDF and the quality of extraction required. Scanned PDFs may require OCR (Optical Character Recognition) to extract text.\n\n## C. DirectoryLoader\n\n**Definition:** DirectoryLoader is used to load multiple documents from a directory or folder.\n\nInstead of creating a separate loader for every file, we can use DirectoryLoader to load multiple files together.\n\n- It can load files matching a specified pattern.\n- We can specify which document loader should be used to process those files.\n- For example, we can use PyPDFLoader to load all PDF files from a directory.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.document_loaders import DirectoryLoader\nfrom langchain_community.document_loaders import PyPDFLoader\n\nloader = DirectoryLoader(\n    \"documents/\",\n    glob=\"**/*.pdf\",\n    loader_cls=PyPDFLoader\n)\n\ndocs = loader.load()\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `documents/` is the directory containing the files.\n- `glob=\"**/*.pdf\"` selects PDF files, including those in subdirectories.\n- `loader_cls=PyPDFLoader` tells DirectoryLoader to use PyPDFLoader to process the selected files.\n- `load()` loads the documents and returns a list of Document objects.\n\n### Limitation of DirectoryLoader\n\nWhen we load a large number of files using `load()`, all the resulting Document objects are collected in memory.\n\nThis can consume a lot of memory when working with large datasets.\n\n### Solution: Lazy Loading\n\nLangChain provides lazy loading to handle large collections of documents more efficiently.\n\n**What is Lazy Loading?**\n\nLazy loading means documents are loaded and yielded one at a time instead of collecting all documents in memory at once.\n\n- It uses a generator/iterator to provide Document objects.\n- We can process each document as it is yielded.\n- It helps reduce memory usage when working with large collections of documents.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\ndocs = loader.lazy_load()\n\nfor doc in docs:\n    print(doc.page_content)\n```\n\n**Important Difference:**\n\n- `load()` returns a list of Document objects.\n- `lazy_load()` returns an iterator that yields Document objects one at a time.\n\nLazy loading does not automatically delete documents from memory if we store them ourselves. Its benefit is that it avoids collecting the entire collection before processing begins.\n\n## D. WebBaseLoader\n\n**Definition:** WebBaseLoader is a document loader in LangChain used to load and extract text content from web pages using their URLs.\n\n- It uses BeautifulSoup to parse HTML and extract visible text.\n- It is useful for loading information from blogs, news articles, and public websites.\n- The extracted content is converted into Document objects.\n- The page URL and other available information can be stored in metadata.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.document_loaders import WebBaseLoader\n\nloader = WebBaseLoader(\n    \"https://example.com\"\n)\n\ndocs = loader.load()\n```\n\n### When to Use?\n\n- Blogs\n- News articles\n- Public websites\n- Websites where the content is primarily text-based and available in the HTML.\n\n### Limitations\n\n- It does not handle JavaScript-heavy pages well.\n- It generally extracts content available in the HTML returned by the web request.\n- It may not capture content that appears only after JavaScript runs in the browser.\n\n**Alternative:** For JavaScript-heavy websites, SeleniumURLLoader can be used because it loads pages through a browser controlled by Selenium.\n\n## E. CSVLoader\n\n**Definition:** CSVLoader is used to load data from CSV files and convert it into Document objects.\n\n- It generally creates one Document object for each row in the CSV file.\n- The values from each row are converted into text and stored in `page_content`.\n- The source file and row information can be stored in metadata.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.document_loaders import CSVLoader\n\nloader = CSVLoader(\n    file_path=\"students.csv\"\n)\n\ndocs = loader.load()\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose a CSV file contains 100 data rows.\n\nCSVLoader generally creates 100 Document objects, one for each row.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Common Methods of Document Loaders\n\nDocument Loaders commonly provide methods to load documents in different ways.\n\n| Method | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `load()` | Loads documents and returns them as a list. |\n| `lazy_load()` | Yields documents one at a time using an iterator. |\n| `aload()` | Asynchronously loads documents. |\n| `alazy_load()` | Asynchronously yields documents one at a time. |\n\nNot every loader supports every method in exactly the same way, so check the specific loader's documentation when needed.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Important Points to Remember\n\n1. Document Loaders convert data from different sources into LangChain Document objects.\n2. A Document object contains two main fields: `page_content` and `metadata`.\n3. A loader's `load()` method generally returns a list of Document objects.\n4. `lazy_load()` yields Document objects one at a time and can help reduce memory usage.\n5. PyPDFLoader generally creates one Document object per PDF page.\n6. CSVLoader generally creates one Document object per CSV row.\n7. DirectoryLoader can load multiple files from a directory using a specified loader.\n8. WebBaseLoader extracts text from web pages using their HTML.\n9. Scanned PDFs may require OCR before their text can be extracted.\n10. Document Loaders are the first step in the typical RAG document-processing pipeline.\n\n**Overall:** Document Loaders make data from different sources available in a common format so that the remaining RAG components can process it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Text Splitters in LangChain\n\n## 1. What are Text Splitters?\n\nText Splitters are components in LangChain used to divide large documents into smaller, manageable pieces called **chunks**.\n\nWhen we work with large documents, processing the entire document at once can be difficult because language models and embedding models have input-size limits.\n\nTherefore, we divide large documents into smaller chunks so that they can be processed efficiently.\n\n## 2. Why Do We Need Text Splitting?\n\n### 1. Model Input Size Limitations\n\n- Every language model and embedding model has a maximum input size.\n- Large documents may exceed these limits.\n- Text splitting divides large documents into smaller chunks that can be processed within these limits.\n\n### 2. Better Downstream Tasks\n\nText splitting improves the quality and efficiency of LLM-powered tasks, such as:\n\n- **Embedding generation:** Smaller chunks can be converted into embeddings individually.\n- **Semantic search:** We can retrieve specific relevant chunks instead of searching through an entire large document.\n- **Question Answering:** The LLM receives relevant chunks as context instead of the complete document.\n- **Summarization:** Large documents can be processed in smaller sections.\n\n### 3. Optimizing Computational Resources\n\n- Processing smaller chunks can make memory and processing requirements more manageable.\n- We can process documents in batches.\n- We avoid unnecessarily sending large amounts of irrelevant information to the LLM.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Important Parameters of Text Splitting\n\nBefore understanding the different splitting techniques, we should understand two important parameters.\n\n## A. Chunk Size\n\nChunk size defines the maximum size of each chunk.\n\nFor example, if we set:\n\n`chunk_size = 100`\n\nIt means that each chunk should contain approximately 100 units according to the configured length measurement.\n\nBy default, many LangChain character-based splitters measure size using characters.\n\n**Note:** Chunk size does not always mean the number of words or tokens. It depends on the splitter and its length function.\n\n## B. Chunk Overlap\n\nChunk overlap defines how much text should be repeated between two consecutive chunks.\n\n**Why do we need it?**\n\nSuppose a sentence gets divided between two chunks. The second chunk might not have enough information to understand the complete meaning.\n\nOverlap helps preserve some context from the previous chunk.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Chunk 1: I am learning Python and want to become a backend developer.\n- Chunk 2: want to become a backend developer and build real-world projects.\n\nHere, some text is repeated in both chunks.\n\nIf we set:\n\n`chunk_overlap = 5`\n\nIt means we aim to keep an overlap of 5 units, according to the splitter's length measurement.\n\n**Important Points:**\n\n- Too little overlap may cause loss of context at chunk boundaries.\n- Too much overlap creates unnecessary repetition and increases the number of chunks.\n- A very large chunk may contain multiple unrelated topics.\n- A very small chunk may not contain enough context to understand the information.\n\nWe should choose chunk size and overlap according to our data and use case.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Types of Text Splitting Techniques\n\nThere are four commonly discussed approaches:\n\n1. Length-Based Text Splitting\n2. Text Structure-Based Splitting\n3. Document Structure-Based Splitting\n4. Semantic Meaning-Based Splitting\n\n## A. Length-Based Text Splitting\n\n**Definition:** In this technique, we divide text into chunks based on a predefined size.\n\nWe decide the maximum chunk size beforehand.\n\nFor example, we can set each chunk to contain approximately 100 characters.\n\n### How does it work?\n\n- We decide the chunk size.\n- The text is divided according to the specified size or separator-based rules.\n- The resulting chunks are kept within the configured size as far as possible.\n\n### Problem with Length-Based Splitting\n\nSuppose we are splitting a paragraph into chunks of 100 characters.\n\nThe splitting process may divide text:\n- Between two words.\n- In the middle of a sentence.\n- Between related pieces of information.\n\nThis can affect the semantic meaning of the chunks.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Original sentence:**\n\n\"Machine learning allows computers to learn patterns from data and make predictions.\"\n\nA poor split could produce:\n\n- Chunk 1: Machine learning allows computers to learn patterns from\n- Chunk 2: data and make predictions.\n\nThe second chunk has lost some of its context.\n\n### Solution: Chunk Overlap\n\nWe can use chunk overlap to preserve some context between consecutive chunks.\n\n### Common LangChain Splitters\n\n**1. CharacterTextSplitter**\n\nSplits text using a specified separator and combines the resulting pieces into chunks according to the chunk-size limit.\n\nSyntax:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import CharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext_splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(\n    separator=\" \",\n    chunk_size=100,\n    chunk_overlap=20\n)\n\nchunks = text_splitter.split_text(text)\n```\n\nImportant parameters:\n\n- `separator`: Defines where the text should be split.\n- `chunk_size`: Maximum target size of each chunk.\n- `chunk_overlap`: Amount of overlapping text between chunks.\n\n**2. TokenTextSplitter**\n\nSplits text based on tokens instead of characters.\n\nThis is useful when we want to control chunk sizes according to the token limits of a model.\n\nSyntax:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import TokenTextSplitter\n\ntext_splitter = TokenTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=100,\n    chunk_overlap=20\n)\n\nchunks = text_splitter.split_text(text)\n```\n\n**Important:** Character-based and token-based splitting are not the same. One character can produce different numbers of tokens depending on the text.\n\n---\n\n## B. Text Structure-Based Splitting\n\n**Definition:** Text Structure-Based Splitting uses the natural structure of written text to divide it into meaningful chunks.\n\nNormally, text is organized hierarchically:\n\n- Paragraphs\n- Sentences\n- Words\n- Characters\n\nInstead of directly cutting text at a fixed position, we try to split it at meaningful boundaries.\n\n### Recursive Character Text Splitting\n\nLangChain provides `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter` for this approach.\n\n**Definition:** RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter tries to divide text using a list of separators in a specified order.\n\nIt first tries to split using the largest natural text boundaries. If the resulting chunks are still too large, it moves to smaller boundaries.\n\n### How Does It Work?\n\nBy default, it uses the following separators:\n\n1. `\"\\n\\n\"` → Paragraph boundary.\n2. `\"\\n\"` → Line boundary.\n3. `\" \"` → Word boundary.\n4. `\"\"` → Character boundary.\n\nSuppose we have a large document.\n\n**Step 1:** It first tries to split the document at paragraph boundaries (`\\n\\n`).\n\n**Step 2:** If a paragraph is still larger than the specified chunk size, it tries splitting it at line boundaries (`\\n`).\n\n**Step 3:** If the resulting pieces are still too large, it tries splitting at spaces (` `), which generally separates words.\n\n**Step 4:** If a piece is still too large, it can split it at the character level.\n\nThis process continues until the chunks fit the specified size as far as possible.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose we have the following text:\n\n```text\nMachine learning is a branch of AI.\n\nIt allows computers to learn patterns from data.\n\nDeep learning is a subset of machine learning.\n```\n\nIf we set a small chunk size, the splitter first tries to keep paragraphs together.\n\nIf a paragraph exceeds the chunk size, it tries smaller boundaries such as lines and words.\n\nThis helps preserve the natural flow of the text as much as possible.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=100,\n    chunk_overlap=20\n)\n\nchunks = text_splitter.split_text(text)\n```\n\n**Important Parameters:**\n\n- `chunk_size`: Maximum target size of a chunk.\n- `chunk_overlap`: Amount of overlap between consecutive chunks.\n- `separators`: List of separators used to split the text.\n- `length_function`: Function used to measure the size of a chunk. By default, it counts characters.\n\n**Why is it called Recursive?**\n\nBecause it repeatedly tries smaller separators whenever the current splitting level does not produce chunks small enough.\n\n**Important:** RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter is a commonly recommended starting point for splitting ordinary text because it tries to preserve natural text boundaries.\n\n---\n\n## C. Document Structure-Based Splitting\n\n**Definition:** This technique is used when documents have their own specific structure or format.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Markdown files\n- Python or other programming-language code\n- HTML documents\n- JSON documents\n\nInstead of treating everything as ordinary text, we use the structure of the document to decide where to split.\n\n### 1. Markdown Text Splitting\n\nMarkdown documents have headings such as:\n\n- `#` → Main heading\n- `##` → Subheading\n- `###` → Smaller heading\n\nWe can use these headings to divide a document into meaningful sections.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```markdown\n# Machine Learning\n\nMachine learning allows computers to learn from data.\n\n## Supervised Learning\n\nIt uses labelled data for training.\n\n## Unsupervised Learning\n\nIt uses unlabelled data to find patterns.\n```\n\nWe can split this document based on its Markdown headings.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter\n\nheaders_to_split_on = [\n    (\"#\", \"Main Topic\"),\n    (\"##\", \"Sub Topic\")\n]\n\ntext_splitter = MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter(\n    headers_to_split_on=headers_to_split_on\n)\n\ndocs = text_splitter.split_text(markdown_text)\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `headers_to_split_on` specifies which Markdown headings should be used.\n- The second value in each tuple becomes the metadata key.\n- `split_text()` splits the Markdown text and returns Document objects.\n\n### 2. Code Splitting\n\nWhen working with programming code, splitting randomly can separate related code.\n\nFor example, a function may get divided in the middle, making it difficult to understand its purpose.\n\nLangChain provides language-specific splitters that try to respect the structure of programming languages.\n\n**Example: Python Code**\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter, Language\n\ntext_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter.from_language(\n    language=Language.PYTHON,\n    chunk_size=100,\n    chunk_overlap=20\n)\n\nchunks = text_splitter.split_text(python_code)\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `Language.PYTHON` specifies that the input is Python code.\n- The splitter uses Python-specific separators.\n- It tries to preserve code structures such as classes and functions.\n\n**Important:** Language-specific splitting attempts to respect code structure, but it does not guarantee that every chunk will be a complete, independently executable function or class.\n\n### Why Do We Need Document Structure-Based Splitting?\n\n- It preserves the logical organization of documents.\n- It keeps related information together.\n- It is useful when processing code, Markdown, HTML, and JSON.\n- It can improve retrieval because chunks retain meaningful document structure.\n\n---\n\n## D. Semantic Meaning-Based Splitting\n\n**Definition:** Semantic splitting divides text based on its meaning rather than relying only on a fixed character count or predefined separators.\n\n### Why Do We Need Semantic Splitting?\n\nSometimes the previous techniques may not produce meaningful chunks.\n\nFor example, suppose a document contains:\n\n**Paragraph 1:** Information about machine learning and its applications.\n\n**Paragraph 2:** Information about deep learning and neural networks.\n\n**Paragraph 3:** Information about cricket and its rules.\n\nA normal length-based splitter might combine unrelated information or separate related information.\n\nEven recursive splitting mainly considers separators and chunk size. It does not actually understand the meaning of the text.\n\nSemantic splitting tries to solve this problem by using embeddings to identify changes in meaning.\n\n### How Does Semantic Splitting Work?\n\n1. The text is divided into sentences.\n2. An embedding model converts sentences or groups of sentences into numerical vectors.\n3. The semantic similarity between neighboring pieces of text is calculated.\n4. When a significant change in meaning is detected, a chunk boundary is created.\n5. Related sentences are grouped together into semantically meaningful chunks.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose a document contains:\n\n- Sentences 1–3: Discuss machine learning.\n- Sentences 4–6: Discuss deep learning.\n- Sentences 7–9: Discuss cricket.\n\nSemantic splitting tries to identify these changes in topic and create chunks accordingly.\n\nUnlike fixed-size splitting, the resulting chunks do not necessarily have equal sizes.\n\n### SemanticChunker in LangChain\n\nLangChain provides `SemanticChunker` for semantic-based splitting.\n\nIt uses an embedding model to calculate semantic differences between sentences and identify where chunks should be separated.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_experimental.text_splitter import SemanticChunker\nfrom langchain_openai import OpenAIEmbeddings\n\nembeddings = OpenAIEmbeddings()\n\ntext_splitter = SemanticChunker(\n    embeddings=embeddings\n)\n\ndocs = text_splitter.create_documents([text])\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `SemanticChunker` performs semantic-based splitting.\n- `OpenAIEmbeddings()` provides the embedding model.\n- `create_documents()` splits the text and returns Document objects.\n\n**Note:** SemanticChunker requires an embedding model, so it may involve additional computation and API costs.\n\n### Important Point\n\nSemantic splitting is useful when preserving the meaning and topic of the text is more important than maintaining equal-sized chunks.\n\nHowever, semantic splitting does not guarantee perfect topic boundaries. Its quality depends on the embedding model and the content being processed.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Comparison of Text Splitting Techniques\n\n| Technique | How It Splits | Best Used For |\n|---|---|---|\n| Length-Based | Based on predefined size. | Simple text and fixed-size requirements. |\n| Text Structure-Based | Based on paragraphs, lines, sentences, and words. | Ordinary text documents. |\n| Document Structure-Based | Based on document-specific structure. | Markdown, code, HTML, JSON. |\n| Semantic-Based | Based on changes in meaning. | Documents containing different topics or concepts. |\n\n# 6. Important Points to Remember\n\n1. Text Splitters divide large documents into smaller chunks.\n2. Chunk size determines the maximum target size of each chunk.\n3. Chunk overlap preserves some context between consecutive chunks.\n4. CharacterTextSplitter uses a specified separator to divide text.\n5. TokenTextSplitter splits text according to token count.\n6. RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter tries multiple separators, starting with larger text boundaries.\n7. MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter divides Markdown documents based on headings.\n8. Language-specific RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter can be used for programming code.\n9. SemanticChunker uses embeddings to identify meaningful changes in text.\n10. The goal of text splitting is to create chunks that are small enough to process but large enough to preserve useful context.\n\n**Final takeaway:** Good chunking is important in RAG because the quality of retrieved information depends partly on how the original documents are divided into chunks.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Vector Stores in LangChain\n\n## 1. Why Do We Need Vector Stores?\n\nLet's understand the need for vector stores through a movie recommendation website similar to IMDb.\n\n### Step 1: Storing movie information\n\nSuppose we are building a movie website where users can explore different movies.\n\nWe need a database to store information about each movie, such as:\n\n- Movie ID\n- Movie name\n- Director\n- Actors\n- Genre\n- Release date\n- Rating\n- Plot or storyline\n\nInitially, we can use this information to display movies on our website.\n\nNow, suppose we want to add a **movie recommendation system** to increase user engagement.\n\n### Step 2: The problem with keyword-based recommendations\n\nOne simple approach is to recommend movies by matching their attributes.\n\nFor example, if two movies have the same actor, director, or genre, we might consider them similar.\n\nHowever, this approach has some limitations.\n\n**Problem 1: Movies can have matching keywords but different storylines.**\n\nFor example:\n\n- *My Name Is Khan*\n- *Kabhi Alvida Naa Kehna*\n\nBoth movies feature Shah Rukh Khan and were directed by Karan Johar. They also share some broad genre characteristics.\n\nHowever, their plots and central themes are quite different.\n\nA keyword-based system might recommend one simply because their attributes match, even when their stories are not very similar.\n\n**Problem 2: Movies can have different keywords but similar themes.**\n\nFor example:\n\n- *Taare Zameen Par*\n- *A Beautiful Mind*\n\nThese movies have different actors, directors, and storylines. A keyword-matching system may consider them unrelated.\n\nHowever, both explore themes related to learning, individual struggles, and how people experience the world differently.\n\nA system based only on matching attributes may fail to identify these thematic similarities.\n\n### Step 3: Comparing movie plots using embeddings\n\nInstead of comparing only movie attributes, we can compare the actual movie plots.\n\nFor this, we can use **embeddings**.\n\nAn embedding model converts text into a numerical vector that captures semantic information about the text.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Movie A's plot → Embedding vector A\n- Movie B's plot → Embedding vector B\n\nWe can then calculate the similarity between these vectors using a similarity measure such as cosine similarity.\n\nThis allows us to find movies with similar meanings or themes, even when they use different words or have different actors and directors.\n\n### Step 4: Challenges with storing and searching embeddings\n\nNow we have two major challenges.\n\n**Challenge 1: Storing embeddings**\n\nSuppose our website contains 10,000 movies.\n\nWe need to generate an embedding for every movie plot.\n\nGenerating embeddings repeatedly whenever a user requests recommendations would be inefficient and could increase processing time and API costs.\n\nTherefore, we should:\n\n1. Generate embeddings when a movie is added or its plot is updated.\n2. Store the generated embeddings.\n3. Reuse them whenever we need to find similar movies.\n\nThis requires a system that can store and manage embedding vectors.\n\n**Challenge 2: Searching for similar embeddings efficiently**\n\nSuppose a user watches Movie A and we want to recommend similar movies.\n\nA basic approach would be:\n\n1. Take Movie A's embedding.\n2. Calculate its similarity with every other movie's embedding.\n3. Sort the results by similarity score.\n4. Recommend the most similar movies.\n\nHowever, if our database contains millions of movies, comparing the query embedding against every stored embedding can become computationally expensive.\n\nWe need an efficient way to search for similar vectors.\n\n### Step 5: The solution — Vector Stores\n\nA **vector store** is a specialized system that stores and manages vector embeddings and provides methods to search for similar vectors.\n\nIt helps us solve both challenges:\n\n1. **Storage:** It stores embeddings along with their associated text/documents and metadata.\n2. **Similarity search:** It provides methods to find vectors that are similar to a given query vector.\n\nMany vector stores use specialized indexing and approximate nearest-neighbor (ANN) search techniques to make similarity searches faster, especially for large collections.\n\nInstead of generating embeddings repeatedly and manually comparing every vector, we can use the vector store's built-in functionality.\n\n---\n\n## 2. How Does a Vector Store Work?\n\nConsider our movie recommendation example.\n\n1. We collect movie plots.\n2. An embedding model converts each plot into a vector.\n3. We store the vectors, movie information, and relevant metadata in a vector store.\n4. When a user watches a movie, we retrieve its embedding.\n5. We search the vector store for similar vectors.\n6. The vector store returns the most relevant matching movies.\n7. We display those movies as recommendations.\n\n**Important:** The vector store performs similarity search, but it does not independently understand which movies a user will enjoy. A complete recommendation system may also need user preferences, ratings, viewing history, and additional ranking logic.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Vector Stores in LangChain\n\nLangChain provides a common interface for working with different vector stores.\n\nThis means that different vector store implementations expose similar methods and follow a common interface.\n\nFor example, we can work with different vector store integrations without having to completely change how we interact with them.\n\nSome popular vector store options include:\n\n- Chroma\n- FAISS\n- Pinecone\n- Weaviate\n- Qdrant\n- Milvus\n\nThey differ in their storage capabilities, deployment options, indexing techniques, and additional features.\n\nKey Features\n\n1. Storage\n\nStores vectors and their associated metadata.\n\nDepending on the implementation, data may be stored in memory or on disk.\n\n2. Similarity Search\n\nHelps retrieve vectors that are most similar to a given query vector.\n\nCommon similarity or distance measures include:\n\nCosine similarity\n\nEuclidean distance\n\nDot product\n\n3. Indexing\n\nProvides data structures or methods that enable efficient similarity searches on high-dimensional vectors.\n\nFor example, Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search.\n\n4. CRUD Operations\n\nCRUD stands for:\n\nCreate: Add new vectors.\n\nRead: Retrieve stored vectors or documents.\n\nUpdate: Update existing entries.\n\nDelete: Remove outdated entries.\n\n3. Vector Store vs Vector Database\n\nThe terms Vector Store and Vector Database are closely related and are sometimes used interchangeably. However, there is a useful conceptual distinction.\n\nVector Store\n\nA vector store typically refers to a lightweight library or service that focuses on:\n\nStoring vector embeddings.\n\nPerforming similarity searches.\n\nRetrieving associated documents and metadata.\n\nIt may not provide all traditional database features, such as advanced transaction management, distributed architecture, or role-based access control.\n\nExample: FAISS\n\nFAISS is primarily a vector search library. It can store vectors in an index and perform similarity searches, but persistence and other database responsibilities generally need to be handled separately.\n\nVector Database\n\nA vector database is a full-fledged database system designed to store and query vector embeddings.\n\nAlong with vector storage and similarity search, it may provide additional database features:\n\nDistributed architecture: Supports horizontal scaling across multiple machines.\n\nPersistence and durability: Supports retaining data, replication, backups, and recovery.\n\nMetadata management: Supports metadata storage, filtering, and sometimes schemas.\n\nTransactions: May provide ACID or near-ACID guarantees, depending on the system.\n\nSecurity: Can provide authentication, authorization, and access control.\n\nVector databases are generally designed for production applications and larger datasets.\n\nExamples:\n\nPinecone\n\nQdrant\n\nWeaviate\n### Common Vector Store Methods\n\nThe following are some important methods commonly used through LangChain's vector store interface.\n\n| Method | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `add_documents()` | Adds LangChain Document objects to the vector store. |\n| `add_texts()` | Adds text along with optional metadata. |\n| `similarity_search()` | Finds documents similar to a query. |\n| `similarity_search_with_score()` | Finds similar documents and returns their scores. |\n| `delete()` | Deletes stored documents or vectors using their IDs. |\n| `from_documents()` | Creates a vector store from a collection of Document objects. |\n| `from_texts()` | Creates a vector store directly from a collection of texts. |\n\n*Exact method availability and behavior can vary between vector store integrations.*\n\n### Understanding the important methods\n\n**1. `add_documents()`**\n\nUsed when we already have LangChain Document objects.\n\nEach Document contains:\n- `page_content`: The actual text.\n- `metadata`: Additional information such as source, movie ID, or page number.\n\n**2. `similarity_search()`**\n\nUsed to search for documents that are semantically similar to a given query.\n\nFor example, we can search for movies with plots similar to a particular movie's plot.\n\n**3. `similarity_search_with_score()`**\n\nSimilar to `similarity_search()`, but also returns a score associated with each result.\n\nThe score's interpretation depends on the vector store and its distance metric. Do not automatically assume that a higher score always means greater similarity.\n\n---\n\n## 4. A Simple LangChain Example\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.documents import Document\nfrom langchain_chroma import Chroma\nfrom langchain_openai import OpenAIEmbeddings\n\n# Create an embedding model\nembeddings = OpenAIEmbeddings()\n\n# Prepare movie documents\nmovies = [\n    Document(\n        page_content=\"A young boy struggles with learning difficulties.\",\n        metadata={\"movie\": \"Movie A\"}\n    ),\n    Document(\n        page_content=\"A brilliant mathematician struggles with mental illness.\",\n        metadata={\"movie\": \"Movie B\"}\n    )\n]\n\n# Create a vector store\nvector_store = Chroma.from_documents(\n    documents=movies,\n    embedding=embeddings\n)\n\n# Search for similar movies\nresults = vector_store.similarity_search(\n    query=\"A person struggling with personal challenges\",\n    k=2\n)\n\nfor result in results:\n    print(result.page_content)\n    print(result.metadata)\n```\n\n**Understanding the parameters:**\n\n- `documents`: The documents we want to store.\n- `embedding`: The embedding model used to convert text into vectors.\n- `query`: The text we want to search for.\n- `k`: The maximum number of similar documents to return.\n\nIn this example, the vector store handles embedding generation during insertion and uses the embedding model to process the search query.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Important Points to Remember\n\n1. A traditional database stores structured information such as movie names, actors, directors, and release dates.\n2. An embedding model converts text into numerical vectors.\n3. A vector store stores and manages these vectors.\n4. A vector store provides similarity-search functionality.\n5. Similarity search helps us retrieve semantically related information.\n6. Vector stores can use indexing techniques to make searching more efficient.\n7. LangChain provides a common interface for interacting with different vector store implementations.\n\n**In one line:**\n\nA vector store is a system used to store embeddings and efficiently retrieve semantically similar data using vector similarity search.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Retrievers in LangChain\n\n## 1. What Is a Retriever?\n\nA **Retriever** is a component in LangChain that fetches relevant documents from a data source in response to a user's query.\n\nIts main purpose is to find and return information that is relevant to the query.\n\nFor example, suppose a user asks:\n\n\"What are the benefits of regular exercise?\"\n\nA retriever searches its available data source and returns documents containing relevant information about exercise and its benefits.\n\n### Important Points\n\n- A retriever fetches relevant documents based on a user's query.\n- It can retrieve information from different data sources.\n- It returns a list of LangChain `Document` objects.\n- A retriever does not necessarily generate an answer. Its job is to retrieve relevant information.\n- Retrievers are commonly used in RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipelines.\n\n**Important:** In LangChain, retrievers implement the Runnable interface. This means we can use methods such as `invoke()` and `ainvoke()` to execute them.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Types of Retrievers\n\nRetrievers can be classified in two ways:\n\n1. Based on their data source.\n2. Based on their retrieval strategy.\n\nThese classifications describe different aspects of retrieval. A retriever can use a particular data source and also apply a particular retrieval strategy.\n\n---\n\n# Part A: Based on Data Source\n\n## 3. Wikipedia Retriever\n\nA Wikipedia Retriever queries Wikipedia to fetch relevant information based on a user's query.\n\n**Important:** It is not the same as a document loader.\n\nA document loader loads documents from a source, whereas a Wikipedia Retriever searches for relevant information in response to a query.\n\n### How Does It Work?\n\nSuppose the user asks:\n\n\"What is machine learning?\"\n\nThe Wikipedia Retriever generally follows these steps:\n\n1. Receives the user's query.\n2. Searches Wikipedia using the Wikipedia API.\n3. Finds relevant Wikipedia pages.\n4. Fetches the content of the relevant pages.\n5. Converts the retrieved content into LangChain Document objects.\n6. Returns the relevant documents to the user or the next component in the pipeline.\n\nIt does not download or load the entire Wikipedia database. It searches for relevant pages based on the query.\n\n### Example\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.retrievers import WikipediaRetriever\n\n# Create a Wikipedia retriever\nretriever = WikipediaRetriever(\n    top_k_results=3,\n    lang=\"en\"\n)\n\n# Search Wikipedia\ndocs = retriever.invoke(\"What is machine learning?\")\n\nfor doc in docs:\n    print(doc.page_content)\n    print(doc.metadata)\n```\n\n**Important parameters:**\n\n- `top_k_results`: Maximum number of Wikipedia results to retrieve.\n- `lang`: Language of the Wikipedia edition to search.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Vector Store Retriever\n\nA **Vector Store Retriever** allows us to search and fetch documents from a vector store based on semantic similarity.\n\nIt uses embeddings to find documents that are semantically related to the user's query.\n\n### How Does It Work?\n\nSuppose we have stored documents about machine learning, deep learning, and artificial intelligence in a vector store.\n\nThe user asks:\n\n\"How do computers learn from data?\"\n\nThe retriever follows these steps:\n\n1. Receives the user's query.\n2. Uses an embedding model to convert the query into a numerical vector.\n3. Searches the vector store for similar vectors.\n4. Retrieves the corresponding documents.\n5. Returns the relevant documents.\n\nThe vector store compares the query embedding with the stored embeddings using its configured similarity or distance measure.\n\n### Example\n\n```python\n# Create a retriever from an existing vector store\nretriever = vector_store.as_retriever(\n    search_kwargs={\"k\": 3}\n)\n\n# Retrieve relevant documents\ndocs = retriever.invoke(\"How do computers learn from data?\")\n```\n\n**Important parameters:**\n\n- `search_kwargs`: Passes search-specific arguments to the vector store.\n- `k`: Number of documents to retrieve.\n\n### Difference Between Vector Store and Vector Store Retriever\n\n- **Vector Store:** Stores embeddings and provides vector similarity-search functionality.\n- **Vector Store Retriever:** Provides a retriever interface to fetch documents from that vector store using a query.\n\nA vector store can perform searches directly, while a vector store retriever makes it convenient to use that search functionality within a LangChain retrieval pipeline.\n\n---\n\n# Part B: Based on Retrieval Strategy\n\n## 5. Maximum Marginal Relevance (MMR) Retriever\n\n### What Is MMR?\n\nMMR stands for **Maximum Marginal Relevance**.\n\nMMR is an information retrieval strategy designed to reduce redundancy in retrieved results while maintaining high relevance to the user's query.\n\nIt tries to answer this question:\n\n\"How can we pick results that are not only relevant to the query but also different from each other?\"\n\n### Why Do We Need MMR?\n\nIn regular similarity search, we may get documents that are:\n\n- Very similar to each other.\n- Repeating the same information.\n- Lacking diverse perspectives.\n\nFor example, suppose we search for:\n\n\"Climate change\"\n\nOur vector store contains these documents:\n\n| Document ID | Content |\n|---|---|\n| D1 | Climate change is causing glaciers to melt rapidly in the Arctic region. |\n| D2 | Glaciers in the Arctic are melting at an alarming rate due to rising temperatures. |\n| D3 | Deforestation in the Amazon is accelerating global climate change. |\n| D4 | Climate change is increasing the frequency of wildfires in California. |\n| D5 | Rising sea levels due to climate change threaten coastal cities like Mumbai and New York. |\n\nA normal similarity search might return:\n\n1. D1: Arctic glaciers melting.\n2. D2: Arctic glaciers melting.\n3. D3: Deforestation in the Amazon.\n\nNotice that D1 and D2 communicate almost the same information.\n\nWe may want results covering different aspects of climate change instead.\n\n### How Does MMR Work?\n\nMMR balances two things:\n\n1. **Relevance:** How relevant is a document to the original query?\n2. **Diversity:** How different is the document from the documents already selected?\n\nIt generally works as follows:\n\n1. First, select the most relevant document.\n2. Then select the next document that is relevant to the query but less similar to the already selected documents.\n3. Repeat until the required number of documents has been selected.\n\nFor our example, MMR might return:\n\n1. D1: Arctic glaciers melting.\n2. D4: Wildfires in California.\n3. D5: Rising sea levels in coastal cities.\n\nInstead of returning multiple documents about Arctic glaciers, it provides information about different aspects of climate change.\n\n### How Does MMR Decide?\n\nMMR uses a scoring function that balances relevance and diversity.\n\nA commonly used formulation is:\n\n**MMR = λ × Relevance − (1 − λ) × Redundancy**\n\nWhere:\n\n- `λ` controls the balance between relevance and diversity.\n- A higher `λ` gives more importance to relevance.\n- A lower `λ` gives more importance to diversity.\n\n**Why is MMR useful?**\n\n- Reduces duplicate or repetitive results.\n- Provides more diverse information.\n- Improves the quality of retrieved context.\n- Particularly useful in RAG pipelines where we want relevant but non-redundant documents.\n\n### MMR in LangChain\n\nVector stores that support MMR search can expose it through their retriever interface.\n\n```python\nretriever = vector_store.as_retriever(\n    search_type=\"mmr\",\n    search_kwargs={\n        \"k\": 3,\n        \"fetch_k\": 10,\n        \"lambda_mult\": 0.5\n    }\n)\n\ndocs = retriever.invoke(\"Climate change\")\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n\n- `search_type=\"mmr\"`: Selects MMR retrieval.\n- `k`: Number of final documents to return.\n- `fetch_k`: Number of candidate documents initially fetched before selecting the final results.\n- `lambda_mult`: Controls the balance between relevance and diversity. Lower values favor diversity; higher values favor relevance.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Multi-Query Retriever\n\n### Why Do We Need Multi-Query Retrieval?\n\nSometimes a single query may not capture all the ways information is expressed in documents.\n\nFor example:\n\nUser query:\n\n\"How can I stay healthy?\"\n\nThis query could mean:\n\n- What should I eat?\n- How often should I exercise?\n- How can I manage stress?\n- What lifestyle habits improve mental and physical wellness?\n- How can I improve my immune system?\n\nA simple similarity search might miss documents that discuss these topics without using the exact word \"healthy\".\n\n### What Is a Multi-Query Retriever?\n\nA Multi-Query Retriever uses an LLM to generate multiple alternative versions of the user's original query.\n\nIt performs retrieval for each generated query and combines the results.\n\n### How Does It Work?\n\n1. Takes the original user query.\n2. Uses an LLM to generate multiple semantically different versions of that query.\n3. Performs retrieval for each generated query.\n4. Combines the retrieved documents.\n5. Removes duplicate documents and returns the combined results.\n\n### Example\n\nOriginal query:\n\n\"How can I stay healthy?\"\n\nThe LLM might generate:\n\n1. \"What are the best foods to maintain good health?\"\n2. \"How often should I exercise to stay fit?\"\n3. \"What lifestyle habits improve mental and physical wellness?\"\n4. \"What can I do to improve my immune system?\"\n5. \"What daily routines support long-term health?\"\n\nThe retriever searches for documents using these alternative queries and combines the results.\n\n### Why Is It Useful?\n\n- Handles ambiguous or broad queries.\n- Captures different interpretations of a question.\n- Finds relevant information expressed in different ways.\n- Improves retrieval coverage.\n\n**Important:** Multi-Query Retrieval improves the chances of finding relevant documents, but it does not guarantee that every generated query or retrieved result will be correct.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Contextual Compression Retriever\n\n### What Is Contextual Compression?\n\nThe Contextual Compression Retriever in LangChain improves retrieval quality by compressing retrieved documents and keeping only the content relevant to the user's query.\n\nIn simple words, it removes irrelevant information from retrieved documents.\n\n### Why Do We Need It?\n\nSuppose the user asks:\n\n\"What is photosynthesis?\"\n\nA traditional retriever returns this paragraph:\n\n\"The Grand Canyon is a famous natural site. Photosynthesis is how plants convert light into energy. Many tourists visit every year.\"\n\n### Problem\n\nThe retriever returns the entire paragraph.\n\nHowever:\n\n- Only one sentence is relevant to the query.\n- The remaining content is irrelevant.\n- This irrelevant information takes up space in the LLM's context window and may distract the model.\n\n### What Does Contextual Compression Do?\n\nIt keeps only the relevant part:\n\n\"Photosynthesis is how plants convert light into energy.\"\n\n### How Does It Work?\n\n1. Receives the user's query.\n2. Uses a base retriever to retrieve candidate documents.\n3. Passes the retrieved documents to a document compressor.\n4. The compressor identifies or extracts the content relevant to the query.\n5. Returns the compressed documents.\n\nThe compressor may use an LLM, an embedding-based filter, or another filtering/extraction technique.\n\n### Why Is It Useful?\n\n- Removes irrelevant information.\n- Reduces unnecessary context.\n- Helps make better use of the LLM's context window.\n- Can improve the quality of the context passed to the LLM.\n\n**Important:** Contextual compression does not necessarily reduce every document to a shorter version. It can also filter out entire irrelevant documents.\n\n---\n\n# 8. More Retrievers in LangChain\n\nApart from the retrievers discussed above, LangChain provides several other retrieval approaches.\n\n### 1. BM25 Retriever\n\nBM25 is a keyword-based information retrieval algorithm.\n\nIt ranks documents based on how well their terms match the search query.\n\nUnlike vector retrieval, BM25 does not require embeddings.\n\n**Useful when:** Exact keywords, names, technical terms, or phrases are important.\n\n### 2. Parent Document Retriever\n\nParent Document Retriever helps retrieve larger parent documents using smaller child chunks.\n\nHow it works:\n\n1. Splits large documents into smaller child chunks.\n2. Stores the child chunks in a vector store.\n3. Searches for relevant child chunks.\n4. Uses their IDs to retrieve the corresponding parent documents.\n5. Returns the larger parent documents.\n\n**Why is it useful?**\n\nSmall chunks can improve retrieval precision, while larger parent documents provide more surrounding context.\n\n### 3. Self-Query Retriever\n\nSelf-Query Retriever uses an LLM to convert a natural-language query into a structured query containing:\n\n- A semantic search query.\n- Metadata filters.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Find science-fiction movies released after 2020.\"\n\nThe retriever can identify:\n- Semantic query: science-fiction movies.\n- Metadata filter: release year greater than 2020.\n\nIt then searches using the query and metadata filter.\n\n**Useful when:** Documents contain metadata such as dates, categories, authors, or ratings.\n\n### 4. MultiVector Retriever\n\nMultiVector Retriever allows multiple vector representations to be associated with a single parent document.\n\nFor example, a document may have:\n\n- Multiple smaller text chunks.\n- A summary.\n- Other generated representations.\n\nThese representations can be embedded and stored for retrieval.\n\nWhen a matching representation is found, the retriever can return the original parent document.\n\n**Useful when:** One representation is not sufficient to retrieve a document effectively.\n\n### 5. Time-Weighted Vector Retriever\n\nThis retriever considers both semantic similarity and recency.\n\nIt gives importance to recently accessed or updated documents, while still considering their relevance.\n\n**Useful when:** Recent information should receive more importance, such as in systems that retrieve recent conversations or activity.\n\n### 6. Ensemble Retriever\n\nAn Ensemble Retriever combines the results of multiple retrievers.\n\nFor example, it can combine:\n\n- BM25 Retriever (keyword-based search).\n- Vector Store Retriever (semantic search).\n\nIt merges their results, often using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF), to produce a combined ranking.\n\n**Useful when:** We want to benefit from both keyword matching and semantic search.\n\n### 7. Arxiv Retriever\n\nArxiv Retriever searches research papers available through the arXiv service.\n\nIt can retrieve relevant research papers based on a user's query.\n\n**Useful when:** Building applications that need to search academic research papers.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Quick Comparison of Important Retrievers\n\n| Retriever | Main purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Wikipedia Retriever | Fetches relevant information from Wikipedia. |\n| Vector Store Retriever | Retrieves semantically similar documents from a vector store. |\n| MMR Retriever | Returns relevant and diverse documents. |\n| Multi-Query Retriever | Generates multiple queries to improve retrieval coverage. |\n| Contextual Compression Retriever | Filters or extracts query-relevant content from retrieved documents. |\n| BM25 Retriever | Retrieves documents using keyword-based ranking. |\n| Parent Document Retriever | Retrieves larger parent documents using matching child chunks. |\n| Self-Query Retriever | Converts natural-language queries into semantic searches with metadata filters. |\n| MultiVector Retriever | Uses multiple vector representations for the same document. |\n| Time-Weighted Vector Retriever | Considers relevance and recency. |\n| Ensemble Retriever | Combines results from multiple retrievers. |\n| Arxiv Retriever | Retrieves academic research papers from arXiv. |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Important Points to Remember\n\n1. A retriever fetches relevant documents in response to a user's query.\n2. Retrievers implement the Runnable interface in LangChain.\n3. A retriever returns documents; it does not necessarily generate the final answer.\n4. A Vector Store Retriever uses embeddings to retrieve semantically similar documents.\n5. MMR balances relevance and diversity to reduce repetitive results.\n6. Multi-Query Retriever uses an LLM to generate alternative queries and combines their retrieval results.\n7. Contextual Compression Retriever removes irrelevant content from retrieved documents.\n8. BM25 performs keyword-based retrieval and does not require embeddings.\n9. Parent Document Retriever searches smaller chunks but can return their larger parent documents.\n10. Ensemble Retriever combines multiple retrieval methods.\n\n### Quick Revision\n\n- **Retriever:** Fetches relevant documents.\n- **Wikipedia Retriever:** Searches Wikipedia.\n- **Vector Store Retriever:** Uses vector similarity search.\n- **MMR:** Relevance + Diversity.\n- **Multi-Query:** One query → Multiple alternative queries → Combined results.\n- **Contextual Compression:** Retrieve first → Compress/filter afterward.\n- **BM25:** Keyword-based retrieval.\n- **Ensemble:** Combines multiple retrievers.\n\n**In one line:** A retriever is responsible for finding the most useful information from a data source so that the next component, such as an LLM, can use it to generate an answer.","metadata":{}}]}